WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.410
Now, you can't plan ahead.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:02.970
But this is an opportunity.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:08.700
How many times you get an opportunity, it's DA DA DA DA DA DA! Do the song, right?

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.250
That Bond song from Your Eyes Only feels like a Bill Conti song to me.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.760
You know, we hear the bassoon.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.200
We hear those moments that make you think that's Bill Conti.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:18.690
Good.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.860
Well, that kind of identity, if it happens,has to do with technique.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.320
David Rose, you know who, David?

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.470
Of course I do.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:36.680
Well, he wrote The Stripper, Holiday for Strings, Little House on the Prairie, Bonanza.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.200
So after my Rocky moment, these composers that I admired,

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.630
I was now allowed into their company, right?

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.040
So David says, You know, what's going to happen to you?

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.950
I said, No, David, what?

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.810
He says "Well, I've been called a string man."

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.430
Holiday For Strings. He says, they want something weepie with strings.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.240
They think of me.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.720
When people think of or they want something brass and heroic,

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.160
They're going to think of you.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.670
And those are the kind of pictures you're going to get.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.140
Even though you're able to do many others.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.990
Hollywood, he says, likes to stamp you as a brand.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:22.030
Now, I'm just quoting what he said. That's what he felt, that's for sure.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.230
It's always about the brave person who who who dares to do something different

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.130
That changes fashion in the first blast.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.890
You do that with the Rocky score.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.140
You do that with that For Your Eyes Only score.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.090
Those because those and those song,

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.220
Because those songs are getting poppier and poppier.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.390
In a contemporary way that was a date stamp on those movies.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:42.820
That song, Sheena Easton singing, suddenly it feels.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.590
like it could be a, it could be a throwback to something like to Laura or that kind of thing.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.070
It was..there was.. in fact, it was one of the more lush and romantic Bond songs that I ever heard.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:57.070
If I could interject like a Dusty Springfield,

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:58.750
You wish Dusty Springfield.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.780
So that's what I was going for, something like that.

